# Reading Session JSON → Parsing → Adding Headers Downstream (Spring Cloud Gateway WebFlux)

The right tool here is a **`GlobalFilter`**, because you want this to run on *every* routed request. The key WebFlux rule: `exchange.getSession()` returns a `Mono<WebSession>`, so everything must stay reactive — compose with `flatMap`, **never** call `.block()`.

The full flow is: **read session → parse JSON → extract fields → mutate request with new headers → forward downstream**.

---

## 1. The DTO you parse into

```java
import java.util.List;

public class SessionInfo {
    private String userId;
    private String tenantId;
    private List<String> roles;

    // no-arg constructor required by Jackson
    public String getUserId() { return userId; }
    public void setUserId(String userId) { this.userId = userId; }

    public String getTenantId() { return tenantId; }
    public void setTenantId(String tenantId) { this.tenantId = tenantId; }

    public List<String> getRoles() { return roles; }
    public void setRoles(List<String> roles) { this.roles = roles; }
}
```

---

## 2. The Global Filter

Replace `"SESSION_JSON"` with the actual attribute name under which your JSON is stored in the session.

```java
import com.fasterxml.jackson.core.JsonProcessingException;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.filter.GatewayFilterChain;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.filter.GlobalFilter;
import org.springframework.core.Ordered;
import org.springframework.http.HttpHeaders;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.server.reactive.ServerHttpRequest;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.util.StringUtils;
import org.springframework.web.server.ServerWebExchange;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

import java.util.List;

@Component
public class SessionToHeaderGlobalFilter implements GlobalFilter, Ordered {

    private static final String SESSION_JSON_ATTRIBUTE = "SESSION_JSON";

    private static final String USER_ID_HEADER   = "X-User-Id";
    private static final String TENANT_ID_HEADER = "X-Tenant-Id";
    private static final String ROLES_HEADER     = "X-User-Roles";

    private final ObjectMapper objectMapper;

    public SessionToHeaderGlobalFilter(ObjectMapper objectMapper) {
        this.objectMapper = objectMapper;
    }

    @Override
    public Mono<Void> filter(ServerWebExchange exchange, GatewayFilterChain chain) {
        return exchange.getSession()                          // Mono<WebSession>
                .flatMap(session -> {
                    Object rawValue = session.getAttribute(SESSION_JSON_ATTRIBUTE);

                    // No session data -> continue untouched
                    if (rawValue == null) {
                        return chain.filter(exchange);
                    }

                    SessionInfo info;
                    try {
                        info = parseSessionInfo(rawValue);   // JSON -> Object
                    } catch (Exception ex) {
                        // Pick your policy: reject, log+continue, or 500.
                        // Rejecting is safest so identity is never silently dropped.
                        return unauthorized(exchange);
                    }

                    // Mutate the request -> add trusted headers
                    ServerHttpRequest mutatedRequest = exchange.getRequest()
                            .mutate()
                            .headers(headers -> {
                                // Strip any client-supplied copies first (anti-spoofing)
                                headers.remove(USER_ID_HEADER);
                                headers.remove(TENANT_ID_HEADER);
                                headers.remove(ROLES_HEADER);

                                setIfPresent(headers, USER_ID_HEADER, info.getUserId());
                                setIfPresent(headers, TENANT_ID_HEADER, info.getTenantId());

                                List<String> roles = info.getRoles();
                                if (roles != null && !roles.isEmpty()) {
                                    headers.set(ROLES_HEADER, String.join(",", roles));
                                }
                            })
                            .build();

                    // Forward the mutated exchange downstream
                    return chain.filter(
                            exchange.mutate().request(mutatedRequest).build());
                });
    }

    private SessionInfo parseSessionInfo(Object rawValue) throws JsonProcessingException {
        // If the attribute is a raw JSON String
        if (rawValue instanceof String json) {
            return objectMapper.readValue(json, SessionInfo.class);
        }
        // If Spring Session already deserialized it into a Map / JsonNode / object
        return objectMapper.convertValue(rawValue, SessionInfo.class);
    }

    private static void setIfPresent(HttpHeaders headers, String name, String value) {
        if (StringUtils.hasText(value)) {
            headers.set(name, value);
        }
    }

    private static Mono<Void> unauthorized(ServerWebExchange exchange) {
        exchange.getResponse().setStatusCode(HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED);
        return exchange.getResponse().setComplete();
    }

    @Override
    public int getOrder() {
        // Run early so the headers are present before routing/forwarding
        return Ordered.HIGHEST_PRECEDENCE + 10;
    }
}
```

`ObjectMapper` is already a Spring bean, so it autowires in directly.

---

## Why each piece matters

**1. Stay reactive — never `.block()`.** `exchange.getSession()` gives a `Mono<WebSession>`. Compose with `flatMap` so you don't stall the Netty event loop.

**2. Requests are immutable — mutate, don't set.** You can't add a header to the incoming request directly. Build a new request via `.mutate().headers(...).build()`, then a new exchange via `exchange.mutate().request(...).build()`, and pass *that* down the chain.

**3. Handle both attribute shapes.** Depending on how the value was stored, the session attribute may be a raw JSON **String** (use `readValue`) or an already-deserialized **Map/object** (use `convertValue`). The `parseSessionInfo` helper covers both.

**4. Header values are plain strings.** Flatten the object into individual headers (as above). If a downstream service genuinely needs the whole structure, forward the JSON itself: `.header("X-Session-Info", json)`.

**5. Order matters.** A high precedence (`HIGHEST_PRECEDENCE + 10`) ensures this runs before the routing filter so the headers are applied before the request is forwarded.

---

## Security notes (don't skip these)

- **Prevent spoofing:** always `remove(...)` any client-supplied copies of your identity headers *before* setting them from trusted session data. Otherwise a caller can send their own `X-User-Id` and impersonate someone.
- **Keep headers small:** put compact identifiers (`X-User-Id`, `X-Tenant-Id`, `X-User-Roles`) rather than secrets, tokens, or large JSON blobs.

---

## Multi-instance gateways

If you run more than one gateway instance, the in-memory `WebSession` won't be shared. Back it with a distributed store using **Spring Session + Redis** so the session is readable on every request regardless of which instance handles it:

```properties
spring.session.store-type=redis
```

with the `spring-session-data-redis` dependency. Spring Session provides the reactive `WebSession` integration needed for WebFlux.

