## If you only want this on *some* routes

Swap the `GlobalFilter` for an `AbstractGatewayFilterFactory` and reference it per-route under `filters:` in `application.yml`. The session-reading/parsing logic inside is identical — only the wiring changes.

**The three things to adapt for your case:** (a) the session attribute name, (b) which JSON fields map to which headers, and (c) your error policy when the JSON is missing or malformed.







Yes. You can have **multiple `GlobalFilter`s** in Spring Cloud Gateway.

Spring will collect all `GlobalFilter` beans, combine them with route-specific `GatewayFilter`s, and sort them using `Ordered` / `@Order`. The lowest order value runs earlier in the **pre** phase and later in the **post** phase. ([cloud.spring.io](https://cloud.spring.io/spring-cloud-gateway/reference/html/))

Example:

```java
@Component
public class ExistingGlobalFilter implements GlobalFilter, Ordered {

    @Override
    public Mono<Void> filter(ServerWebExchange exchange, GatewayFilterChain chain) {
        // existing logic
        return chain.filter(exchange);
    }

    @Override
    public int getOrder() {
        return 0;
    }
}
```

And your new session/header filter:

```java
@Component
public class SessionHeaderGlobalFilter implements GlobalFilter, Ordered {

    @Override
    public Mono<Void> filter(ServerWebExchange exchange, GatewayFilterChain chain) {
        return exchange.getSession()
                .flatMap(session -> {
                    // read session, parse JSON, mutate headers
                    return chain.filter(exchange);
                });
    }

    @Override
    public int getOrder() {
        return -100;
    }
}
```

Because `-100` is lower than `0`, `SessionHeaderGlobalFilter` runs **before** `ExistingGlobalFilter` in the request/pre phase.

---

You can also use `@Order` instead of implementing `Ordered`:

```java
@Component
@Order(-100)
public class SessionHeaderGlobalFilter implements GlobalFilter {

    @Override
    public Mono<Void> filter(ServerWebExchange exchange, GatewayFilterChain chain) {
        return exchange.getSession()
                .flatMap(session -> {
                    // logic
                    return chain.filter(exchange);
                });
    }
}
```

---

So yes, you can keep your existing `GlobalFilter` and add another one.

Just be careful with the ordering:

```java
public int getOrder() {
    return Ordered.HIGHEST_PRECEDENCE + 10;
}
```

Use a very early order if the session-derived headers must be available to later filters and downstream routing.

